/* book Worked example: Q: An ideal vapour-compression refrigerator uses R-134a between 0.14 MPa and 0.8 MPa, with a flow of 0.05 kg/s. Table values: saturated vapour at 0.14 MPa (−18.8 °C), h₁ = 387.3 kJ/kg, s₁ = 1.7402 kJ/(kg·K); at 0.8 MPa and s = 1.7402, h₂ = 423.5 kJ/kg (39.0 °C); saturated liquid at 0.8 MPa (31.3 °C), h₃ = 243.6 kJ/kg. Find the refrigerating effect, compressor power, condenser duty, COP, and compare with the Carnot COP between −18.8 °C and 31.3 °C.
   1. Throttle: h₄ = h₃ = 243.6 kJ/kg (the mixture entering the evaporator has quality 0.32).
   2. Refrigerating effect: Qin = ṁ(h₁ − h₄) = 0.05 × (387.3 − 243.6) = 0.05 × 143.7 = 7.18 kW.
   3. Compressor: Win = ṁ(h₂ − h₁) = 0.05 × (423.5 − 387.3) = 0.05 × 36.2 = 1.81 kW.
   4. Condenser: Qout = ṁ(h₂ − h₃) = 0.05 × 179.9 = 9.00 kW = Qin + Win.
   5. COPR = 7.18/1.81 = 3.97.
   6. Carnot: TC = 254.4 K, TH = 304.5 K, so COP = 254.4/50.1 = 5.08. The ideal vapour cycle reaches 3.97/5.08 = 0.78 of it; the throttle and the superheated discharge account for the gap.
   Answer: Qin = 7.18 kW, Win = 1.81 kW, Qout = 9.00 kW, COPR = 3.97 against a Carnot COP of 5.08. */
