/* book Worked example 2: Q: A Linde plant compresses nitrogen to 200 bar at 300 K and returns the unliquefied gas at 1 atm and 300 K. From a nitrogen table: h₁ − h₂ = 32.1 kJ/kg (gas at 1 atm and 300 K minus gas at 200 bar and 300 K) and h₁ − hf = 433.2 kJ/kg (hf is saturated liquid at 1 atm, 77 K). Find the fraction liquefied and the liquid made per 100 kg of gas compressed.
   1. Energy balance on the cold box (exchanger, valve and separator, adiabatic, no work): ṁh₂ = y ṁ hf + (1 − y) ṁ h₁.
   2. Solve: y = (h₁ − h₂)/(h₁ − hf) = 32.1/433.2 = 0.0741.
   3. Per 100 kg compressed: 7.4 kg of liquid nitrogen; 92.6 kg goes back to the compressor.
   4. h₂ < h₁ at the same 300 K means nitrogen cools on throttling at room temperature (positive Joule–Thomson coefficient), so the simple Linde cycle works for it.
   Answer: y = 0.074: about 7.4 kg of liquid per 100 kg of nitrogen compressed. */
