/* book: lead: Entropy measures how many microscopic arrangements fit the same macroscopic state. Two samples can have the same energy, volume and number of particles yet very different numbers of ways to arrange those particles. Boltzmann turned that count into a number: S = kB ln Ω. The idea exists because the Clausius definition from entropy and the Clausius inequality tells you how entropy changes, but not w */
/* book Figure 3.10.1: The worked example: four labelled molecules in a box with two halves. The macrostate with n molecules on the left can be made in 1, 4, 6, 4 or 1 ways; the even split has the most microstates and so the largest entropy, S = k ln 6. */
