/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A microstate is one exact arrangement of the particles in a system.
   - Take four labelled molecules and a box split into two equal halves.
   - The basic assumption of statistical thermodynamics is that, at fixed U, V and N, every accessible microstate is equally likely.
   - S is entropy in J/K; kB = 1.381 × 10⁻²³ J/K is the Boltzmann constant; ln is the natural logarithm; Ω is the number of accessible microstates at fixed U, V and N, dimensionless and at least 1.
   - The logarithm makes entropy additive.
   - When Ω = 1, ln 1 = 0 and S = 0.
   - At absolute zero a perfect crystal has every atom in its lowest-energy position.
   - Some real solids never reach that single arrangement.
   - Entropy is not merely 'disorder'.
*/
