/* book exam traps: Entropy is not just 'disorder'; it is the count of accessible microstates at fixed U, V and N. A visually messy system can have low entropy if few arrangements are accessible. | Ω is a count of microstates, not a probability and not work. It is dimensionless and cannot be less than 1. | R = NA kB. Use S = kB ln Ω for the whole system; per mole, S = R ln w only when each molecule independently has w arrangements (CO in a crystal, w = 2), because then Ω = wNA. | S = 0 at 0 K only for a perfect crystal with a unique ground state. Glasses, CO and ice can keep residual entropy. | Do not say 'entropy is the number of microstates'; entropy is proportional to ln Ω, so it is not Ω itself. Doubling Ω does not double S. */
